<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-9-29">
  <title>Resources for 9/29</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain what it means for a sequence <m>\{ x_n \}</m> to converge to a real number $L$.  This means both knowing the precise mathematical definition for use in proving theorems about the limits of sequences, as well as being able to connect the formal definition of convergence with our intuitive understanding of the limit of a sequence.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given a sequence <m>\{ x_n \}</m>, use your intuition or other methods to guess the limit.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given a sequence <m>\{ x_n \}</m> and a guess at its limit, use the formal definition of convergence to verify the limit.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use tools like the triangle inequality to prove theorems about the convergence of sequences.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m>, think flexibly about quantities like <m>\frac{\epsilon}{2}</m> or <m>\frac{\epsilon}{3}</m>, being able to see these numbers as playing the role of <q><m>\epsilon</m></q> in the definition of convergence.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use the flexibility described in the previous objective to prove theorems about the convergence of sequences.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-intro-sequences">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Introduction to Sequences</title>
    <p>
      Below are links to a video that is an <em>introduction to sequences</em>, which is available both on Google Drive and YouTube. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="G44ntA-phnY" />

    <example>
      <title>Introduction to Sequences</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Determine what the following converge to, if anything.  Prove your result.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\{ \frac{2n}{3n+2} \right\}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\{ \frac{n^2}{n^2+2} \right\}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Suppose that <m>\{ x_n \}</m> is a bounded increasing sequence.  Must the sequence converge? If so, to what?  What about bounded decreasing sequences?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Define the sequence <m>\{ a_n \}</m> by <m>a_1=\frac12</m>, and <m>a_{n+1}=\sqrt{a_n}</m> for all <m>n \geq 2</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove that <m>a_n &lt; 1</m> for all <m>n \geq 1</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove that <m>\{ a_n \}</m> is monotone.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What can you conclude?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  (Not on quiz) Can you find a sneaky way to find the limit of the sequence?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-general-sequence">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for General Sequence Theorems</title>
    <p>
      Below are links to a video on <em>general sequence theorems</em>, which is available both on Google Drive and YouTube. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="G44ntA-phnY" />

    <example>
      <title>General Sequence Theorems</title>
      <p>
        Assume <m>\{a_n\}</m> converges to <m>a</m> and <m>\{ b_n \}</m> converges to <m>b</m>.  Determine what the following converge to and prove your result.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\{ a_n -b_n \}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\{ ca_n \}</m>, where <m>c \in \mathbb{R}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\{ a_nb_n \}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\{ \frac{1}{b_n} \}</m>, where each <m>b_n \ne 0</m> and <m>b \ne 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\{ \frac{a_n}{b_n} \}</m>, where each <m>b_n \ne 0</m> and <m>b \ne 0</m>. Hint:  Don't use the definition.  Use #3 and #4 above.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

